Thank you for sharing your experience. Your project clearly shows that business continuity planning is not only an IT recovery activity but also an organizational project requiring input from many business areas.
One point that stood out to me was the conflict between Finance and Customer Services when deciding whether ERP or CRM should receive the higher recovery priority. I think this is a good example of why Business Impact Analysis is important. Without agreed criteria such as financial impact, customer impact, regulatory obligations, and maximum tolerable downtime, recovery priorities could easily become based on departmental preference rather than overall business risk.
I also appreciated your discussion of tabletop exercises. In many projects, documentation can give the impression that the organization is prepared, but testing is what reveals whether people actually understand their roles and whether the recovery procedures are realistic.
My question is: after the tabletop exercises identified gaps such as outdated contact information and undocumented manual workarounds, did the team establish a process to keep the Business Continuity Plan updated regularly, or was the update process handled primarily as part of this project?
