QW

My Response:
Hello QW,
Thank you for taking the time to read my post and for raising that question. I'm glad you highlighted the role of the Business Impact Analysis and the tabletop exercises because they were two of the most valuable parts of the project. They helped move the discussion beyond technical recovery and ensured that business decisions were based on operational priorities rather than individual departmental preferences.
To answer your question, the update process did not conclude with the project's completion. One of the project's key deliverables was establishing an ongoing governance process to ensure the Business Continuity Plan remained current and effective. During project closure, management approved a maintenance schedule that included annual reviews of the BCP, updates following significant organizational or technology changes, and periodic tabletop exercises to validate recovery procedures. Each business unit was also responsible for regularly reviewing its recovery procedures and contact information, while the Information Security and Risk Management team coordinated the overall review process.
The findings from the tabletop exercises, including outdated contact information and undocumented manual workarounds, were entered into an action log and assigned to the appropriate business owners for remediation before the project was formally closed. We also recommended that future internal audits and risk assessments include periodic reviews of the Business Continuity Plan to ensure it continued to reflect changes in business processes, technology, regulatory requirements, and emerging risks.
One of the key takeaways from this project was realizing that a Business Continuity Plan should never be viewed as a completed document to be put aside. As the organization changes, whether through new technologies, business processes, or emerging risks, the plan also needs to evolve. Regular reviews, testing, and updates are essential to ensure it remains practical and effective when it is needed most.
I hope this answers your question. Thank you again for your thoughtful question and for contributing to the discussion.
Regards


