Response:
Thank you, WA, for your comment and question.
Determining the recovery priority for systems was one of the most important and challenging aspects of the project. As I mentioned in my original post, different business units initially had competing priorities. For example, Finance considered the ERP system the highest priority because of its role in payroll processing and financial reporting, while Customer Services believed the CRM platform should be restored first due to its direct impact on customer support and revenue.
Rather than allowing departmental preferences to drive the decision, the project manager facilitated additional Business Impact Analysis (BIA) workshops involving representatives from each business unit. Together, we evaluated objective criteria such as operational impact, financial consequences, regulatory obligations, customer service impact, system dependencies, and the maximum tolerable period of disruption. This structured approach enabled stakeholders to reach consensus on the recovery sequence based on business impact rather than individual opinions.
The BIA findings were then used to establish Recovery Time Objectives (RTOs) and Recovery Point Objectives (RPOs) for each critical system, ensuring that recovery priorities aligned with the organization's operational needs and continuity objectives.
This experience reinforced the importance of involving both business and IT stakeholders in continuity planning. While IT teams understand the technical dependencies, business leaders provide the operational context needed to make informed recovery decisions. Bringing both perspectives together resulted in a more practical and effective Business Continuity Plan.
Thank you







